MOS Persistent Cognitive Substrate (PCS)
Specification v0.1
Part II — Logical Storage Model
Abstract

Part II defines the logical organization of persistent cognition.

It specifies how Cognitive Objects are represented, identified, versioned, related, and evolved within the Persistent Cognitive Substrate.

Unlike the Physical Storage Model, this document is entirely implementation independent.

Its purpose is to establish a stable logical contract between cognition and persistence.

Chapter 1
Purpose

The Logical Storage Model bridges cognition and persistence.

It defines:

what persistent objects exist,
how they relate,
how they evolve,
how they maintain identity,
how they preserve history,
how they participate in reasoning.
Chapter 2
Cognitive Object Model (COM)

The Cognitive Object Model is the canonical representation of all persistent cognition.

Every persistent concept SHALL be represented as a Cognitive Object.

Examples include:

Entity
Fact
Observation
Episode
Goal
Plan
Procedure
Skill
Reflection
Policy
Identity
Environment
Simulation
Prediction
Resource
Communication

The COM is independent of storage technology and serves as the universal semantic model.

Chapter 3
Cognitive Object Structure

Every Cognitive Object SHALL contain the following logical components:

Persistent Identity

Object Type

Memory Space

Storage Class

Storage Domain

Version

Lifecycle State

Temporal Context

Provenance

Relationships

Security Context

Metadata

Payload

The payload contains the object-specific content; all other components are standardized across object types.

Chapter 4
Identity Model

Every Cognitive Object SHALL possess a globally unique Persistent Identifier (PID).

Identity SHALL be:

immutable,
globally unique,
stable across versions,
independent of storage location.

No backend-specific identifier SHALL be exposed as the canonical identity.

