Chapter 6
Relationships

Objects SHALL relate through typed references.

Relationship categories include:

Structural
Semantic
Temporal
Causal
Dependency
Ownership
Governance
Derivation
Similarity
Communication

Relationships SHALL themselves be first-class Cognitive Objects.

This allows relationships to carry:

confidence,
provenance,
temporal validity,
metadata.
Chapter 7
Object Composition

Complex Cognitive Objects MAY be composed of other Cognitive Objects.

Example:

Plan

├── Goal

├── Milestone

├── Tasks

├── Procedures

├── Policies

└── Resources

Composition SHALL preserve independent identities for all child objects.

Chapter 8
Versioning Model

Objects evolve through immutable versions.

Object

↓

Version 1

↓

Version 2

↓

Version 3

Each version SHALL preserve:

timestamp,
author,
provenance,
reason for change,
supersession link.

Historical versions remain queryable.

Chapter 9
Object State Machine

Every object progresses through explicit states.

Created

↓

Validated

↓

Active

↓

Referenced

↓

Updated

↓

Consolidated

↓

Archived

↓

Retained

State transitions SHALL be auditable.

Chapter 10
Temporal Model

Each object participates in multiple timelines.

Required temporal attributes include:

Creation Time
Observation Time
Valid Time
Transaction Time
Consolidation Time
Archive Time

Temporal reasoning SHALL preserve all dimensions independently.

Chapter 11
Provenance Graph

Every object SHALL participate in a Provenance Graph.

Example:

Observation

↓

Fact

↓

Reflection

↓

Skill

↓

Decision

This enables complete reconstruction of reasoning pathways.

Chapter 12
Object Dependencies

Objects MAY depend on other objects.

Dependency types include:

Requires
Produces
Consumes
Implements
Extends
References
Invalidates
Supersedes

Dependency cycles SHOULD be detected and managed explicitly.

Chapter 13
Cognitive Cohesion

Objects belonging to the same reasoning process SHOULD maintain logical cohesion.

Related objects MAY be grouped into Cognitive Contexts.

Examples:

Active investigation
Software deployment
Financial analysis
Medical diagnosis

A Cognitive Context provides a reusable boundary for reasoning and retrieval.

Chapter 14
Referential Integrity

The Logical Storage Model requires semantic referential integrity.

References SHALL remain valid across:

version changes,
migrations,
storage relocation,
replication.

Broken references SHALL be detectable.

Chapter 15
Object Evolution

Objects evolve through:

reinforcement,
refinement,
contradiction,
supersession,
consolidation,
reflection.

Evolution SHALL preserve complete historical lineage.

Chapter 16
Explainability

Every object SHALL support reconstruction of:

origin,
evolution,
dependencies,
supporting evidence,
affected decisions,
current confidence.

Explainability is a first-class property of every Cognitive Object.

Chapter 17
Compliance

A Logical Storage Model implementation complies with PCS Part II if it provides:

Cognitive Objects as first-class entities.
Stable object identities.
Explicit object taxonomy.
Typed relationships.
Immutable version history.
Multi-dimensional temporal semantics.
Provenance graphs.
Referential integrity.
Object state machines.
Explainability.
Part II Result

The Logical Storage Model defines what exists inside persistent cognition without specifying how it is stored.

At this point, the architecture has three clear layers:

MOS Core Specification
        │
        ▼
Persistent Cognitive Substrate
        │
        ▼
Logical Storage Model
        │
        ▼
Physical Storage Model
        │
        ▼
Database Backend
A refinement that emerged while writing Part II

One architectural concept became increasingly clear:

Cognitive Contexts

Throughout the RFCs we've talked about Working Memory, Active Context, and Memory Activation. However, those describe runtime cognition.

The Logical Storage Model benefits from a persistent counterpart:

A Cognitive Context is a persistent, addressable collection of Cognitive Objects that together describe a coherent reasoning domain or activity.

Examples include:

"Incident #2026-0812"
"Research Project: Battery Chemistry"
"Customer Support Case #18492"
"Deployment: Production Release v2.4"

A Cognitive Context is not merely a tag or folder. It has its own identity, lifecycle, provenance, relationships, and metadata. It allows MOS to retrieve, version, archive, and explain complex reasoning as a cohesive unit rather than as isolated objects.

I believe Cognitive Contexts will become one of the key building blocks in the MOS API (open_context(), activate_context(), archive_context()) and in the PostgreSQL implementation (through dedicated context tables and optimized indexes). They provide a clean bridge between the dynamic cognition described in RFC-0014 and the persistent storage model defined here.
